-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/69e1fc84-c1c5-56ab-9a2a-baa552fc398f
-- title:
--   Widths, component map and glued specialisation for J₀(Nq) at q
-- statement:
--   Setting. Let $N\ge 1$ and let $q$ be a prime with $q\nmid N$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$ in the sense of [`ValuationSubring.LiesOverPrime`](def/FLTPrelim_Ramification.html#L16), i.e. the image of $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; its residue field $\kappa:=$ `ResidueField A` then carries the characteristic-$q$ structure supplied by [`ValuationSubring.charP_residueField_of_liesOverPrime_def`](def/WeierstrassCurve_ReductionMap.html#L57). Write $F:=$ `modularFunctionFieldC κ N` for the $\kappa$-subfield of the field of Laurent series over $\kappa$ generated by `jqModC κ` and `jqNModC κ N`, and $g:=$ `arithFrobC q κ N` for the semilinear automorphism of $F$ over $\kappa$ obtained from the $q$-power Frobenius of $\kappa$ acting on coefficients (an element of the group `SemilinearAut κ F` of pairs of ring automorphisms of $F$ and of $\kappa$ compatible with the structure map). Places of $F/\kappa$ are objects of `Place κ F`: valuation subrings of $F$ containing $\kappa$, different from $F$, whose ideals are principal. The groups `JZero N` and `JZero (N*q)` are the degree-zero divisor class groups $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the modular function fields of levels $N$ and $Nq$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, equipped with the module structures `heckeModuleBar` over the Hecke algebra $\mathbb{T}:=$ `HeckeAlg` $=\mathbb{Z}[X_\ell:\ell\ \text{prime}]$, whose generators are $T_\ell:=$ `heckeGen ℓ`. Finally $H:=$ `inertiaInvariants A (N*q)` is the subgroup of classes in `JZero (N*q)` fixed by every element of `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, the image in $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $A$.
--
--   Hypotheses. The assertion is made for every finite set $W$ of places of $F/\kappa$ and every pair of hypotheses: `hW`, which requires that a place belongs to $W$ exactly when it lies in `ssPlaces q N κ`, i.e. is a supersingular place (rational, an affine geometric place, and with value of `jGeomGen κ N` in `ssJSet q κ`); and `hstab`, which requires the finite set of node pairs $\Sigma:=$ `nodePairsOfPlaces g W` $=\{(w,g\cdot w):w\in W\}$ to be stable under $g$, in the sense that $(g\cdot s_1,g\cdot s_2)\in\Sigma$ for every $s\in\Sigma$.
--
--   Conclusion. There exist a width function $e:$ `Place κ F` $\to\mathbb{N}$, additive maps
--   $$\mathrm{comp}:H\longrightarrow \Phi_e,\qquad \mathrm{sp}:H\longrightarrow \mathrm{GluedPic}^0(\kappa,F,\Sigma),$$
--   a $\mathbb{T}$-module structure on $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa,F)$, and an additive map $\mathrm{sp}_N:$ `JZero N` $\to\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa,F)$, with the following properties. Here $\Phi_e:=$ `componentGroup (widthOfPlaces g W e)` is the quotient of the $\mathbb{Z}$-dual of the character lattice (the kernel of `degreeOn` inside $\Sigma\to\mathbb{Z}$) by the image of the Gram map of the width function $s\mapsto e(s_1)$ on $\Sigma$; $\mathrm{GluedPic}^0(\kappa,F,\Sigma)$ is the quotient of the group of admissible gluing data (pairs of degree-zero divisors vanishing at the first, respectively second, member of each node pair, together with a family of elements of `Additive κˣ` indexed by $\Sigma$) by the subgroup of glued principal data; and $\nu:=$ `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair Σ` is the induced homomorphism to $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa,F)\times\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa,F)$.
--
--   (i) $e(w)>0$ for every $w\in W$.
--
--   (ii) $\mathrm{comp}$ is surjective.
--
--   (iii) For every prime $\ell\nmid Nq$ and every $x\in H$ with $T_\ell x\in H$: $\mathrm{comp}(T_\ell x)=(\ell+1)\cdot\mathrm{comp}(x)$.
--
--   (iv) For every $T\in\mathbb{T}$ and $x\in H$ with $Tx\in H$: if $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ then $\mathrm{comp}(Tx)=0$.
--
--   (v) For every $\varphi\in\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ which is a Frobenius at $q$ for $A$ in the sense of [`ValuationSubring.IsFrobeniusAt`](def/EllipticCurve_FrobeniusTrace.html#L51) (it lies in the decomposition subgroup and induces $x\mapsto x^q$ on $\kappa$), and every $x\in H$ with $\varphi x\in H$: if $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ then $\mathrm{comp}(\varphi x)=0$.
--
--   (vi) For every prime $\ell\nmid Nq$ and every $x\in H$ with $T_\ell x\in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$: if `HeckeInputsFibre κ N ℓ` holds (existence of principal divisors on the degeneracy roof `charLDegeneracyRoof κ N ℓ`, integrality of the two degeneracy maps, and the fact that the associated correspondence `heckeDivFibre` descends to $\mathrm{Pic}^0$), then $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(T_\ell x))$ is obtained from $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(x))$ by applying the endomorphism `heckePic0Fibre κ N ℓ` to each of its two components.
--
--   (vii) For every $T\in\mathbb{T}$ and $x\in H$ with $Tx\in H$: if $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ and $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(x))=0$, then $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(Tx))=0$.
--
--   (viii) For every Frobenius $\varphi$ at $q$ for $A$ and every $x\in H$ with $\varphi x\in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$: $\mathrm{sp}(\varphi x)=$ `GluedPic0.glueMap Σ g hstab` applied to $\mathrm{sp}(x)$.
--
--   (ix) For every $x\in H$ with $T_q x\in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$, and every family $u:\Sigma\to$ `Additive κˣ` with $\mathrm{sp}(x)=$ `GluedPic0.nodeUnit Σ u`: $\mathrm{sp}(T_qx)=$ `GluedPic0.nodeUnit Σ` applied to $u$ precomposed with the inverse of the permutation `SemilinearAut.nodePerm Σ g hstab` of $\Sigma$.
--
--   (x) For every $x\in H$ which is prime-to-$q$ torsion (`PrimeToTorsion q`: some $n>0$ with $q\nmid n$ kills $x$): if $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ and $\mathrm{sp}(x)=0$ then $x=0$.
--
--   (xi) For every prime $\ell\nmid Nq$ and every $x\in H$ with $T_\ell x\in H$ and $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$: $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(T_\ell x))=T_\ell\cdot\nu(\mathrm{sp}(x))$, the action on the pair being the diagonal one coming from the $\mathbb{T}$-module structure on $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa,F)$ produced above.
--
--   (xii) $\mathrm{sp}_N$ is $\mathbb{T}$-equivariant: $\mathrm{sp}_N(Ty)=T\cdot\mathrm{sp}_N(y)$ for all $T\in\mathbb{T}$ and $y\in$ `JZero N`.
--
--   (xiii) For every $y\in$ `JZero N` which is prime-to-$q$ torsion: $\mathrm{sp}_N(y)=0$ implies $y=0$.
--
--   (xiv) Every prime-to-$q$ torsion class $c\in\mathrm{Pic}^0(\kappa,F)$ is of the form $c=\mathrm{sp}_N(y)$ for some prime-to-$q$ torsion $y\in$ `JZero N`.
--
--   (xv) For every $\sigma\in$ `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` and every prime-to-$q$ torsion class $x\in$ `JZero (N*q)`: $\sigma x-x$ lies in $H$, and $\mathrm{comp}(\sigma x-x)=0$ and $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(\sigma x-x))=0$.
--
--   (xvi) For every $m\in\mathbb{N}$ coprime to $q$ and every $z\in\mathrm{GluedPic}^0(\kappa,F,\Sigma)$ with $mz=0$, there is $x\in H$ with $mx=0$, $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ and $\mathrm{sp}(x)=z$.
--
--   (xvii) For every $m\in\mathbb{N}$ coprime to $q$ and every $\phi\in\Phi_e$ with $m\phi=0$, there is $x\in H$ with $mx=0$ and $\mathrm{comp}(x)=\phi$.
--
--   (xviii) For every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and every $x\in H$ with $\sigma x\in H$: $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ implies $\mathrm{comp}(\sigma x)=0$.
--
--   (xix) For such $\sigma$ and $x$ with $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$: $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(x))=0$ implies $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(\sigma x))=0$.
--
--   (xx) For such $\sigma$ and $x$ with $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$, and all $a,b\in$ `JZero N`: if $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(x))=(\mathrm{sp}_N(a),\mathrm{sp}_N(b))$ then $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(\sigma x))=(\mathrm{sp}_N(\sigma a),\mathrm{sp}_N(\sigma b))$.
--
--   (xxi) There exists a $\mathbb{T}$-module structure on $\Phi_e$ such that: $\mathrm{comp}(Tx)=T\cdot\mathrm{comp}(x)$ for all $T\in\mathbb{T}$ and $x\in H$ with $Tx\in H$; and, for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ of $\mathbb{T}$ such that the $\mathfrak{m}$-torsion `heckeTorsion` of $\Phi_e$ is trivial, every $x$ in the $\mathfrak{m}$-torsion of `JZero (N*q)` which is prime-to-$q$ torsion, lies in $H$, and satisfies $\mathrm{comp}(x)=0$ and $\nu(\mathrm{sp}(x))=0$, belongs to `toricMonodromyPart q (A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ)`, the $\mathbb{T}$-submodule of `JZero (N*q)` spanned by the elements $\sigma y-y$ with $\sigma$ in the inertia group and $y$ killed by some positive integer coprime to $q$.
--
--   This packages, in the language of places and glued divisor class groups, the description of the special fibre at $q$ of the modular curve of level $Nq$ together with its component group, the specialisation of the inertia invariants of $J_0(Nq)$, and the compatibility of both with the Hecke action, the Frobenius and the decomposition group, plus the good-reduction specialisation of $J_0(N)$. It is the common input of [`ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroSemistableSpecialization`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroSemistableSpecialization) and its two refinements, which assemble these data into the semistable-specialisation structure used for the toric monodromy and level-lowering arguments at the prime $q$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_width_comp_sp.lean

import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_JZeroSemistableSpecialization
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_HeckeModule
import Definitions.Def_ValuationSubring_ReduceAt
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_SupersingularNodePlaces
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_CoeffSemilinearAut
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_CharLDegeneracyHecke
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_ToricMonodromyPart

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
